    'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Your bio is the few lines people read right after your photos, and it often decides whether they send a message or keep scrolling. If you have been staring at the empty box wondering how to write a dating profile bio that does not sound boring or boastful, take a breath. You do not need to be a writer. You need to be specific, honest and a little warm.</p>
<p>This guide gives you a simple formula, examples for different personalities, a list of what to include and what to skip, and sample bios you can copy and make your own. It works whether you want a partner, a date or a new friend.</p>

<h2>Why your bio matters more than you think</h2>
<p>Photos get attention, but words build interest. Two people can have equally nice pictures, and the one who has written something real will usually get the conversation. A good bio does three things: it tells people who you are, it hints at what you would enjoy doing together, and it gives them an easy reason to say hello.</p>
<p>Think of it as a short, friendly introduction at a gathering. You would not recite your CV or list everything you dislike. You would say a little about yourself, show some warmth and ask something back. The same approach works on a profile.</p>

<h2>How to write a dating profile bio in four simple steps</h2>
<p>If you are not sure where to begin, use this four-part formula. Each part is one sentence, and together they make a bio that feels complete.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>The hook.</strong> One line that shows your personality. "Nairobi born, weekend adventurer, professional taster of street mandazi."</li>
<li><strong>The passions.</strong> Two or three specific things you enjoy. "I love hiking, gospel and soul music, and cooking for friends on Sundays."</li>
<li><strong>The goal.</strong> What you are here for, in plain words. "Looking for something genuine and long term with someone kind."</li>
<li><strong>The question.</strong> End with something easy to answer. "Tell me your best hidden eating spot in town."</li>
</ol>
<p>Write the four lines first, then read them aloud. If it sounds like something you would actually say to a new friend, you have got it right. If it sounds like a poster or a job application, loosen it up.</p>

<h2>What to include in your bio</h2>
<p>The best bios are full of small, concrete details. "I like travelling" could be anyone. "I am slowly visiting every county, and Lamu is next" is only you. Here is what to reach for:</p>
<ul>
<li>Two or three real interests, named specifically, such as a sport, a cuisine, a genre of music or a hobby.</li>
<li>A hint of your daily life, such as your work, your studies or your side project, without giving away your exact workplace.</li>
<li>Something that shows your sense of humour or your values, whichever feels more natural.</li>
<li>What you are looking for, in one kind sentence.</li>
<li>A conversation hook: a question, a recommendation request or a playful challenge.</li>
</ul>
<p>Specific details do the heavy lifting. They give people something to ask about, and they make your first messages more interesting for you to read too.</p>

<h2>What to skip in your bio</h2>
<p>A few habits quietly push good people away. Try to leave these out:</p>
<ul>
<li>Lists of what you refuse to put up with. It reads as bitter, even if you have been hurt before.</li>
<li>Phrases like "no drama" or "not here to waste time", which sound defensive.</li>
<li>Clichés such as "I love to laugh" or "I am simple and easy-going". Show it instead.</li>
<li>Your phone number, social media handles or exact address. Keep personal contact details private until you trust someone. Our guide to <a href="/blog/protect-your-privacy-on-dating-apps">protecting your privacy on dating apps</a> explains why.</li>
<li>Negative comments about your ex or about the opposite gender.</li>
</ul>
<p>You can state your standards without a list of warnings. "I value honesty and good communication" says the same thing in a way that invites people in rather than fencing them out.</p>

<h2>Bio examples for different personalities</h2>
<p>You do not have to sound bubbly if you are not bubbly. Here is how the formula bends to suit different people.</p>
<p><strong>The quiet one.</strong> "Introvert with a loud laugh once I know you. Happiest with a good book, a pot of chai and one or two close friends. Looking for someone calm and genuine. What are you reading at the moment?"</p>
<p><strong>The funny one.</strong> "I can cook one very good pilau and I will mention it often. Football on weekends, terrible at dancing but I try anyway. Here for good company and good banter. Convince me that your team is better than mine."</p>
<p><strong>The outdoorsy one.</strong> "Hiking Ngong on Saturdays, chasing sunsets at the coast when I can. I am happiest outside and a little muddy. Looking for a partner who likes adventure and slow mornings too. Where is the best trail you have walked?"</p>
<p><strong>The family-minded one.</strong> "Close to my family and proud of it. Sunday lunch is non-negotiable in my house. I am looking for a serious relationship with someone who values kindness and faith. What is the dish that tastes like home to you?"</p>
<p><strong>The career-focused one.</strong> "Busy building my business, but I always make time for good people. I enjoy early-morning runs, podcasts and learning new things. I want a partner who has their own goals and supports mine. What are you working towards this year?"</p>
<p>Notice that none of these use big words or brag about money or status. They are warm, particular and end with a question.</p>

<h2>Ready-to-copy sample bios</h2>
<p>Use these as a starting point. Change the details so they fit your life, because a bio that is copied word for word will not sound like you.</p>
<ul>
<li>"Teacher by day, amateur chef by night. I love matatu playlists, long walks and any film with a good soundtrack. Looking for someone honest and kind to share Sunday afternoons with. What is your comfort food?"</li>
<li>"New in town and still learning the shortcuts. I enjoy live music, board games and trying every chips-and-chicken spot I can find. Would love to meet people for friendship first and see where it goes. Any recommendations?"</li>
<li>"Software developer who still prefers real conversations to screens. I run, I read, I make decent ugali. Here for something genuine with someone who laughs easily. What is the best thing you ate this week?"</li>
<li>"Mother of one, lover of gospel music and good tea. My family keeps me grounded and my friends keep me laughing. Looking for a respectful man who knows what he wants. Tell me something that made you smile today."</li>
<li>"Studying part time, working full time and sleeping when I can. I am looking for friends and maybe more, but mostly for people who make me laugh. Who is your favourite comedian?"</li>
</ul>

<h2>Make your bio easy to reply to</h2>
<p>A bio is not only a description, it is an invitation. Before you save it, read it as a stranger would and ask: what would I message this person about? If you cannot find anything, add a detail. A named hobby, a favourite place, a half-finished goal or a playful opinion all work.</p>
<p>Ending with a question is the simplest trick. It turns your profile from a statement into the start of a chat, and it means the other person does not have to work to think of an opener. If you would like more ideas for what to say once someone replies, see our <a href="/blog/five-first-message-openers">first message openers</a> and the guide to <a href="/blog/how-to-start-a-conversation-online">starting a conversation online</a>.</p>

<h2>Edit, test and refresh it</h2>
<p>Do not worry about getting it perfect on the first try. Write a draft, leave it for a day, then reread it with fresh eyes. Cut anything that sounds like a cliché and add one detail you would be proud to talk about. Ask a trusted friend whether it sounds like you.</p>
